          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em differentiable manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            which is
            %em locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            (a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluing+functions"} gluing functions
            which relate these Euclidean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+charts"} local charts
            to each other are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+functions"} differentiable functions
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            and if one demands
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} analytic
            gluing functions then one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+manifolds"} analytic manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
          %p
            Accordingly, a differentiable manifold is a space to which the tools of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+analysis"}> infinitesimal
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            may be applied
            = succeed "." do
              %em locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            between differentiable manifolds is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiation"} differentiable
            by computing its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            pointwise in any of the Euclidean
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
          %p
            In particular one may consider smooth functions from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            into any smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
            of these that have the same first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            at a given point capture the idea of “infinitsimal smooth paths” through that point in the manifold, called its
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vectors"} tangent vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            constitute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_x X
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated
            to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+bundle"} frame bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Fr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fr(X)
          %p
            By equipping the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+bundle"} frame bundle
            of a differentiable manifold with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+properties"} extra properties
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            one encodes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            on the manifold. For example equipping them with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+structure"} orthogonal structure
            encodes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+geometry"} Riemannian geometry
            on manifolds. More generally one may consider any
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            on the frame bundle and thereby equip the manifold with the corresponding
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+geometry"} conformal geometry
            etc.
          %p
            This way differential and smooth manifolds are the basis for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            are in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            If one relaxes the condition on differentiable manifold from it being locally isomorphic to a Euclidean space to it just admitting local smooth maps from a Euclidean space, then one obtains the more general concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            or even
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
            for more on this. If one generalizes here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+functions"} differentiable functions
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+algebra"} simplicial
            differentiable functions one obtains concepts of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifold"} derived smooth manifold
          %p
            The generalization of differentiable manifolds to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            and more generally
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            then one obtains the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stacks"} smooth stacks
            and eventually
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-stacks"} smooth infinity-stacks
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p For convenience, we first recall the basic definition of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#TopologicalManifolds"} Topological manifolds
          %p and of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#DifferentiableFunctionsBetweenCartesianSpaces"} Differentiable functions between Euclidean spaces
          %p and then turn to the actual definition of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#DifferentiableManifolds"}> Differentiable manifolds
              \.
          %h3#TopologicalManifolds Topological manifolds
          %p
            For convenience, we first recall here some background on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifolds"} topological manifolds
          #TopologicalManifold.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (topological manifold)
            %p
              A
              %em
                topological manifold is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                which is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+Euclidean+topological+space"}> locally Euclidean
                  ,
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+Hausdorff+topological+space"}> paracompact Hausdorff
                  \.
            %p
              If the local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+spaces"} Euclidean
              neighbourhoods
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n \overset{\simeq}{\to} U \subset X
              are all of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              for a fixed
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
